# Cellular Neurobiology
#
# Membrane potentials from ion gradients, the Hodgkin-Huxley action potential as a cycle of channel states, fast sodium inactivation by the IFM motif, synaptic release, and long-term potentiation.

#>neuroscience_cellular
#:layer #!reference
#:domain #!neuroscience
#:form #!state_machine
#:level #!advanced
#:review #!sourced
#:valence #!bonk

^"emit"{
 ~#title: "Cellular Neurobiology"
 ~#claim: "Ion gradients and selective permeability set the membrane voltage; voltage-gated sodium channels open, inactivate through an IFM motif, and recover, and that cycle shapes every action potential."
 ~#proof: "Goldman, Hodgkin, and Katz for resting potentials; Hodgkin and Huxley for the action potential; West and colleagues for the IFM motif; Bliss and Lomo for LTP."
 ~#door: "Read the resting potential, walk the sodium channel states, then follow the synapse into plasticity."
 ~#audience: "advanced students with some physics and cell biology"
 continuity: "Cellular Neurobiology | open, inactivate, and recover"
 cold_open: "Every nerve impulse rides on sodium channels that open, inactivate, and recover, a tiny cycle repeated along the whole axon."
}

